# DESCRIPTION
# A problem that asks the student to find
# the formula of a line through two points.
# ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject('Lines and Linear Functions')
## DBchapter('')
## DBsection('')
## KEYWORDS('lines, linear functions')
## Author('Peter Staab')
## Institution('Fitchburg State University')
## Date('7/10/2022')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',         'PGML.pl',
    'PGstatisticsmacros.pl', 'parserAssignment.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

Context("Numeric");
parser::Assignment->Allow;
parser::Assignment->Function("P");

@pop = (
    [ 1800, 5.308 ],
    [ 1810, 7.239 ],
    [ 1820, 9.638 ],
    [ 1830, 12.866 ],
    [ 1840, 17.069 ],
    [ 1850, 23.191 ],
    [ 1860, 31.443 ],
    [ 1870, 38.925 ],
    [ 1880, 50.189 ],
    [ 1890, 62.979 ],
    [ 1900, 76.212 ],
    [ 1910, 92.228 ],
    [ 1920, 106.021 ],
    [ 1930, 122.775 ],
    [ 1940, 132.164 ],
    [ 1950, 150.697 ],
    [ 1960, 179.323 ],
    [ 1970, 203.392 ],
    [ 1980, 226.545 ],
    [ 1990, 248.709 ],
    [ 2000, 281.421 ],
    [ 2010, 308.745 ],
    [ 2020, 331.449 ]
);

# take 5 random years.
@years = num_sort(random_subset(5, 0 .. $#pop));
@rows = map {$pop[$_]} @years;

$tab = DataTable([@rows], );

$year0 = $pop[ $years[0] ]->[0];

@x = map { $pop[$_]->[0] - $year0 } (@years);
@y = map { $pop[$_]->[1] } (@years);

($m, $b) = linear_regression(~~@x, ~~@y);
$P = Formula("P(x) = $m x + $b");
$f = Formula("$m x + $b");

$rand_year = random($year0 + 2, $pop[ $years[-1] ]->[0] - 2);
$pop2      = $f->eval(x => $rand_year - $year0);

$rand_pop  = random(Round($y[0]) + 2, Round($y[-1]) - 2);
$predict_y = Real(Round(($rand_pop - $b) / $m)) + $year0;

BEGIN_PGML
The population of the United States from some years from the census is given in the table below:

[#
    [.year.][.Population (in millions).]{headerrow => 1}*
    [. .] {rows => ~~@rows}
#]{horizontalrules => 1, align => '|c|c|'}

a) Let [`x`] be the number of years since [$year0].  Find a linear model 
for [`P(x)`], the population (in Millions) that best fits this data.
Write your answer in the form [`P(x) = m x + b`].  ( Use software and 
write the slope and y-intercept to at least 4 decimal places.)

    [_]{$P}{30}

b) Use the linear model developed above to determine population (in Millions) 
of the United States in [$rand_year] [_]{$pop2}{10}

c) Use the linear model developed above to determine the year that the 
U.S. had [$rand_pop] million people. [_]{$predict_y}{10}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) In this case, use software to find the least-square/linear regression to the data in the table.  One should get [`m=[@Round($m,5)@]`] and [`b = [@ Round($b,5)@]`], so the linear model should be [``` [$P] ```]
b) Evaluate the model above for [`x = [$rand_year]-[$year0] = [$rand_year-$year0] `], which is the number of years since [$year0]. 

    [``` P = [@Round($m,5)@] \cdot [$rand_year-$year0] + [@ Round($b,5)@] = [$pop2] ```]

c) In the model above, let [`P=[$rand_pop]`] and solve for [`x`]:

    [```
    \begin{aligned}
    [$rand_pop] & = [@Round($m,5)@] x + [@ Round($b,5)@] \\
    [$rand_pop] - [@ Round($b,5)@] & =  [@Round($m,5)@] x  \\
    \frac{ [@ Round($rand_pop - $b,5)@]}{[@Round($m,5)@]} & = x \\
     & = x
    \end{aligned}
    ```]

    We round this to the nearest whole number so [`x = [@Round(($rand_pop-$b)/$m, 0)@]`] and then to find the year add [$year0] to get [$predict_y].
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
